<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugdunum Convenarum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint-Bertrand-de-Comminges</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lugdunum Convenarum was the Roman town of the Convenae in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> valley of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> around 72 BCE. It had a forum, temples, baths and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and became an important town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Roman Aquitania</a>. It is now Saint-Bertrand-de-Comminges, known for its Romanesque and Gothic cathedral, and gave its name to the region of Comminges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum.jpg" alt="Lugdunum Convenarum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fifistorien · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lugdunum_Convenarum-thermes_forum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
